Task: convert the ASCII diagram in the section "### ASCII" into D2 code.

Output rules
- Output ONLY D2 code. No explanation, no Markdown fence, no comments outside D2.
- Use plain D2 syntax that compiles with "d2 --layout dagre" (d2 v0.9).
- A "$" inside a label starts a D2 variable substitution: put such labels in
  single quotes ('...') or write "\$" – e.g. '$remote_addr' or "\$remote_addr".
- Take the layout direction from the ASCII drawing ("direction: down" for top
  to bottom flows, "direction: right" for left to right flows).

Labels
- Copy every label EXACTLY as written in the ASCII: same words, same case,
  same paths, same symbols. Add nothing, leave nothing out, do not translate.
- Text that stands inside a box belongs to that box: use one quoted label
  with \n line breaks (no Markdown / LaTeX label blocks).
- Text written next to an arrow is the label of that connection.
- A box that contains other boxes or lines of text with "->" becomes a
  container; its title (the text in the top border) is the container label.

Shapes and colours (simple technical style, white background)
- Database / PDB / schema objects: shape cylinder for a database, rectangle
  for schemas inside it; fill "#DEEBF7", stroke "#2E75B6".
- WebLogic / middleware / application server / ORDS / Nginx / Java processes:
  fill "#E2EFDA", stroke "#548235"; a main component may use fill "#A9D18E".
- External systems / clients / browser / scripts / other systems:
  fill "#EDEDED", stroke "#7F7F7F".
- Containers (zones, hosts, groups): fill "#FFFFFF", stroke of the matching
  category colour, style.stroke-dash: 4.
- All shapes: style.border-radius: 8, style.stroke-width: 1, style.shadow:
  false, style.font-size: 20 (container titles 22, bold),
  label colour
  style.font-color "#C00000" for component names (dark red, like the blog
  diagrams); plain descriptive lines inside a box may use "#262626".
- Connections: style.stroke "#7F7F7F", style.stroke-width: 2, label
  font-color "#000000", style.font-size: 18; arrow heads as in the ASCII
  (-> one way, <-> both).
- Connection labels: split into short lines (\n) at natural breaks – after
  the protocol / keyword, after a "/" or before a bracket; at most about 18
  characters per line, up to three lines, e.g.
  "HTTPS\n/ords/<schema>/\n<module>/..." or "proxy:\nORDS_PUBLIC_USER\n[<SCHEMA>]".
  Words stay exactly as written.
- A box from which a labelled connection starts, and which stands next to
  another such box, gets "width: 280" so the labels of the two arrows do not
  overlap.
- Keep the drawing narrow (it is shown in a ~900 px wide page column):
  - a container whose parts are stacked in the ASCII gets "grid-columns: 1"
    and "grid-gap: 16" instead of a direction, so the layout cannot place its
    parts side by side;
  - break long labels into several lines (\n) between words, at most about
    45 characters per line – the words themselves stay exactly as written;
  - connections start and end at the container itself, never at a part
    inside a "grid-columns" container (the layout engine cannot layer edges into
    a grid and then spreads the containers sideways); keep the arrow label exactly – it
    names the target anyway.
- Never set style.3d (D2 allows it only on rectangles – on a cylinder it is
  a compile error); leave it out everywhere.
- No icons, no images, no shadows, no gradients, no themes beyond these
  styles, no legend unless the ASCII has one.

Width limit for THIS diagram (the ASCII is drawn wide; the image must fit a
~900 px page column):
- Arrange it top to bottom: use "direction: down" at the top level, also when
  the ASCII shows the parts side by side (two variants / two modes / two
  machines / a long left-to-right chain).
- Parts that stand side by side in the ASCII (e.g. MODE A and MODE B, machine
  A and machine B, INBOUND and OUTBOUND) become containers placed BELOW each
  other; a long horizontal chain becomes a vertical chain.
- Target width: at most about 1000 px. Keep all labels and connections
  exactly; only the arrangement changes.
